a new report looks at how long small  businesses anticipate these economic  obstacles in addition to what they are  now offering their employees to keep  them  cabbage from American Express pulled 550  Small Business Leaders and the latest  small business recovery report shows  businesses have nearly doubled Revenue  year over year between July of last year  and July of this year but profits did  not follow suit inflation is making a  meaningful dent in their business and so  from that same period they saw  tremendous Revenue growth but their  profits had slightly declined they  actually declined four percent during  that time period Brett Sussman says  these businesses anticipate future  economic obstacles to last at least  until summer of 2023 almost half the  businesses reported that inflation is  impacting their labor market they're  accommodating and compensating for  higher Health Care better employee  benefits and more frequent pay raises  they're in giving raises to their  existing employees in fact 40 percent  said they're looking to increase wages  on existing employees they're actually  providing more workplace flexibility  which you usually really think about for  larger companies and not smaller  companies so 49 said they're putting  practices in place that gives you  flexibilities when analyzing hybrid and  remote working options by business size  and age businesses less than two years  old are more likely to offer this to its  employees and these options are nearly  twice as likely to be implemented among  the largest small businesses compared to  the smallest small businesses Sussman  says it's a great tactic to retain  people and keep costs down  he also adds that small businesses have  to incorporate digital Facebook is  reported as the top platform nearly  doubled out of Instagram followed by  YouTube LinkedIn and Twitter
 

